    <section class="knowledge-section knowledge-section--paper"><div class="knowledge-section-heading"><p class="knowledge-kicker">The foundation</p><h2>What are music royalties and why do they matter?</h2></div><div class="knowledge-columns"><div><p>Music royalties are payments made to rights holders when copyrighted music is used commercially. Depending on the use, payments may be due to songwriters, composers, performing artists, producers, publishers, record labels, master recording owners, and other copyright owners.</p><p>Unlike a performance fee that may be earned once for appearing at a concert, royalties can continue to generate income throughout the commercial life of a song through streaming, radio, television, public performance, licensing, synchronization, and other uses.</p></div><div class="knowledge-highlight"><strong>Music is both a creative work and an intellectual property asset.</strong><p>Royalty income can help finance recordings, marketing, tours, new projects, catalogues, and sustainable music businesses.</p></div></div></section>

    <section class="knowledge-section"><div class="knowledge-section-heading"><p class="knowledge-kicker">The main categories</p><h2>Different uses create different royalties.</h2></div><div class="royalty-type-grid"><article v-for="royalty in royaltyTypes" :key="royalty.title"><strong>{{ royalty.title }}</strong><p>{{ royalty.text }}</p></article></div></section>

    <section class="knowledge-section knowledge-section--mint"><div class="knowledge-section-heading"><p class="knowledge-kicker">One song, several rights</p><h2>Composition and master rights are not the same asset.</h2></div><div class="rights-cards"><article><span>01</span><h3>The composition</h3><p>The underlying music and lyrics. Rights may belong to songwriters, composers, music publishers, or parties who acquired publishing rights.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>The master recording</h3><p>The actual recorded performance. Rights may belong to the artist, record company, producer, investor, or another party depending on the agreement.</p></article></div><p class="knowledge-note">One commercial use can therefore generate income connected to both the composition and the master recording.</p></section>

    <section class="knowledge-section knowledge-section--paper"><div class="knowledge-section-heading"><p class="knowledge-kicker">Calculation and collection</p><h2>How music royalties move through the system.</h2></div><div class="knowledge-columns"><div><p>There is no single universal royalty rate. Calculations can depend on streams, broadcasts, performances, territory, licensing arrangements, subscription type, advertising revenue, ownership percentages, rights category, platform revenue, distribution agreements, and collection procedures.</p><p>Streaming platforms generally allocate subscription, advertising, and licensing revenue through agreements with distributors, labels, publishers, collection organizations, and other rights-management entities. The same number of streams may therefore have different values across services, countries, and reporting periods.</p></div><div class="royalty-flow"><span>Music user</span><b>→</b><span>Licensing organization</span><b>→</b><span>Rights holder</span><span>Streaming platform</span><b>→</b><span>Distributor</span><b>→</b><span>Artist or master owner</span></div></div><div class="knowledge-role-grid"><article><strong>Distributor</strong><p>Delivers recordings to platforms and may provide release management, metadata delivery, revenue collection, statements, catalogue management, international distribution, and analytics.</p></article><article><strong>Music publisher</strong><p>Manages composition rights, registers works, records songwriter ownership, collects eligible royalties, monitors usage, licenses compositions, and administers songwriter income.</p></article></div></section>

    <section class="knowledge-section"><div class="knowledge-section-heading"><p class="knowledge-kicker">Protect the catalogue</p><h2>Ownership splits, split sheets, and metadata matter.</h2></div><div class="knowledge-columns"><div><h3>Why ownership splits matter</h3><p>When several people contribute to a song, ownership should be agreed as early as possible. Whatever arrangement is agreed should be properly documented with contributor names, songwriting percentages, publishing ownership, producer participation, master ownership, signatures, and the date of agreement.</p><h3>Use split sheets</h3><p>A split sheet records who contributed to a song and the percentage assigned to each contributor. It may include legal and professional names, contact details, publishing information, ownership percentages, performing-rights information, and signatures. Written documentation protects everyone involved.</p></div><div class="knowledge-highlight knowledge-highlight--coral"><strong>Metadata is the information behind your music.</strong><p>Artist names, song titles, songwriter and producer credits, publisher information, ownership percentages, ISRC, and release details help royalty systems match usage with the correct rights holder.</p><p>Incorrect or incomplete metadata can contribute to missed matches, delayed payments, and lost revenue opportunities.</p></div></div></section>

    <section class="knowledge-section knowledge-section--mint"><div class="knowledge-section-heading"><p class="knowledge-kicker">For Ghanaian creators</p><h2>Think globally while keeping local records strong.</h2></div><div class="knowledge-columns"><div><p>Ghanaian music can generate value through radio, television, public performances, digital streaming, commercial establishments, international platforms, audiovisual licensing, live performances, and online content.</p><p>Creators should understand which rights they own, which organizations administer those rights, and which parties collect each type of income. A song may generate activity in Ghana, other African countries, Europe, North America, Asia, the Caribbean, and other international markets.</p></div><div><p>Make music available through appropriate global services, keep contracts and rights data organized, and monitor royalty reports for top-performing songs, revenue trends, strongest territories, missing repertoire, incorrect ownership, and new audience markets.</p><p>Technology can improve metadata, analytics, rights management, reporting, catalogue databases, usage monitoring, and digital licensing, but it works best when the underlying rights information is accurate.</p></div></div></section>

